Prepare Minikube
/home/user/devops-practice/k8s

Check whether Minikube is already installed
minikube version

What does this command do?
- If Minikube is installed, it displays the version.
- If it isn't installed, you'll see command not found. That's okay; we'll install it next.

Download Minikube
              curl -LO https://github.com/kubernetes/minikube/releases/latest/download/minikube-linux-amd64

What does it do?
- curl downloads a file from the internet.
- -L follows redirects.
- -O saves the file using its original filename.
- minikube-linux-amd64 is the Minikube executable for 64-bit Intel/AMD Linux systems.

ls -lh minikube-linux-amd64   ----This checks that the downloaded file exists and displays its size

Install Minikube
              sudo install minikube-linux-amd64 /usr/local/bin/minikube


Verify the installation
           minikube version

You now have two important Kubernetes tools ready:
- kubectl — your tool for communicating with Kubernetes.
- minikube — your tool for creating and managing your local Kubernetes cluster.

— Start Kubernetes using Docker
            minikube start --driver=docker

What does this command mean?
Part	Purpose
minikube	Runs the Minikube tool
start	Creates and starts your local Kubernetes cluster
--driver=docker	Uses your existing Docker engine to run the cluster

Verify your Kubernetes cluster
kubectl cluster-info

Check your nodes
           kubectl get nodes

Check the system pods
           kubectl get pods -A

A Pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes. It runs one or more containers.
Some pods may take a little time to become ready, so don't worry if you initially see ContainerCreating.

          kubectl get nodes
          kubectl get pods -A

Next lesson: Hire our first worker
We'll deploy a small web application using Nginx.
In our office analogy:
- Nginx container: the worker serving web pages.
- Pod: the worker's workspace.
- Deployment: management's instruction to keep the required number of workers available.
- Service: the reception desk that gives us a way to reach the application.


           kubectl get namespaces
           kubectl get deployments -A

Deploy our first application

Create a Deployment
       kubectl create deployment office-web --image=nginx:latest

Check the Deployment
       kubectl get deployments
       kubectl get pods

create a Service and access the Nginx welcome page from your laptop.
kubectl expose deployment office-web --type=NodePort --port=80

        Check the Service
         kubectl get services

minikube service office-web --url   ----- This asks Minikube for a URL through which you can reach the Service. Depending on your WSL setup, 
the URL may require a running tunnel or a different access method, so don't assume that simply displaying the URL proves the web page is reachable.

             curl http://127.0.0.1:34731

What happens if the worker disappears? k8s self healing. what happends if a node dies
kubectl delete pod YOUR-POD-NAME
kubectl get pods

           Verify that reception still works
           kubectl get services

Next lesson: Scale our office to three workers
            kubectl scale deployment office-web --replicas=3
            kubectl get deployments
            kubectl get pods

Next lesson: Watch reception direct traffic
            kubectl get service office-web
            kubectl describe service office-web

Let's inspect the selector and worker labels
the Service provides a stable way to reach the application, but it does not guarantee perfectly equal traffic distribution between every replica.
          kubectl get pods --show-labels
          kubectl get endpointslices office-web   ----This gives us another view of the addresses registered for the Service.


Our next office experiment: Remove one worker from the staffing plan  --- (deleting a service)
          kubectl scale deployment office-web --replicas=2
          kubectl get deployments
          kubectl get pods
          kubectl get endpointslices

Save our application configuration in YAML  -- Create our first Kubernetes YAML file        
         nano office-web-deployment.yaml -  paste the yaml manifset file provided by AI

Validate the staffing plan
        kubectl apply --dry-run=client -f office-web-deployment.yaml
                  DRY RUN --- checked the file without applying its changes to the live cluster.
Compare the plan with the running Deployment
        kubectl diff -f office-web-deployment.yaml
             


